<subsection xml:id="subsec-inhomogeneous-higher-order">
  <title>Inhomogeneous Linear ODEs — Method of Undetermined Coefficients</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We now turn to the <em>inhomogeneous</em> linear ODE with constant
      coefficients:
      <me>
        y^{(n)}(t) + a_{n-1}\, y^{(n-1)}(t) + \cdots + a_1\, y'(t)
        + a_0\, y(t) = f(t), \qquad f(t) \not\equiv 0.
      </me>
      The crucial structural fact is the <em>superposition principle</em>:
      the general solution is the sum of the general homogeneous solution
      (which we already know how to find) and <em>any</em> particular
      solution of the inhomogeneous equation.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ─── Superposition principle ─── -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-superposition-principle">
    <title>Superposition Principle</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>y_p(t)</m> be any particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE
        <me>
          y^{(n)} + a_{n-1} y^{(n-1)} + \cdots + a_0 y = f(t).
        </me>
        Let <m>y_h(t) = C_1 y_1(t) + \cdots + C_n y_n(t)</m> be the general
        solution of the corresponding <em>homogeneous</em> ODE (i.e.\ with
        <m>f \equiv 0</m>).
        Then the <term>general solution of the inhomogeneous ODE</term> is
        <me>
          y(t) = y_h(t) + y_p(t),
        </me>
        and every solution of the inhomogeneous ODE has this form for some
        choice of <m>C_1, \ldots, C_n \in \R</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <!-- ─── Three-step method ─── -->

  <!-- ADDED: checklist format makes the workflow explicit and avoids fragile algorithm markup -->
  <assemblage xml:id="alg-undetermined-coefficients">
    <title>Method of Undetermined Coefficients</title>
    <p>
      Building upon the superposition principle (<xref ref="thm-superposition-principle"/>),
      we do not need to find all solutions of the inhomogeneous ODE from scratch.
      Since we already know how to solve the underlying homogeneous ODE, 
      in order to solve an inhomogeneous ODE, it is enough to find one 
      particular solution <m>y_p</m> satisfying the
      equation with the given right-hand side <m>f(t)</m>; then the full
      solution is <m>y=y_h+y_p</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <term>method of undetermined coefficients</term> is one possible way
      to find such a particular solution.  Its guiding intuition is that a
      linear physical system often responds to an external excitation with an
      answer of the same basic structure: a polynomial input suggests a
      polynomial response, an exponential input suggests an exponential
      response, and a sinusoidal input suggests a combination of sine and
      cosine.  We therefore write down a trial function with that structure,
      leave some coefficients unknown, substitute the trial function into the
      ODE, and determine the unknown coefficients algebraically.
    </p>
    <p>
      This leads to the following three-step method for solving an inhomogeneous linear ODE with constant coefficients.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 1 — Solve the homogeneous ODE.</em>
      Find <m>y_h(t) = C_1 y_1(t) + \cdots + C_n y_n(t)</m> using the
      characteristic polynomial.  The root-to-solution translation is
      given by <xref ref="thm-solutions-from-roots"/>, and the form of
      the full homogeneous solution is given by
      <xref ref="thm-general-solution-homogeneous"/>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 2 — Choose a trial function for one particular solution <m>y_p(t)</m>.</em>
      Use the table in <xref ref="thm-undetermined-coefficient-guesses"/>.
      First write the natural trial function suggested by <m>f(t)</m>.  Then check
      whether that same shape already appears among the homogeneous
      solutions.  If it does, multiply the whole trial function by the required
      power of <m>t</m>; this resonance correction is explained in
      <xref ref="rem-resonance-explanation"/>.  Finally substitute
      <m>y_p</m> into the ODE and solve for the unknown constants in the
      trial function.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 3 — Use superposition to write the general solution.</em>
      By <xref ref="thm-superposition-principle"/>,
      <m>y(t) = y_h(t) + y_p(t)</m>.
      Apply initial conditions to determine <m>C_1, \ldots, C_n</m>.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <!-- ─── Trial-function table ─── -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-undetermined-coefficient-guesses">
    <title>Trial Functions for the Particular Solution</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider an inhomogeneous linear ODE with constant coefficients
        <me>
          y^{(n)}(t)+a_{n-1}y^{(n-1)}(t)+\cdots+a_1y'(t)+a_0y(t)=f(t),
        </me>
        and let
        <me>
          P(\lambda)=\lambda^n+a_{n-1}\lambda^{n-1}
          +\cdots+a_1\lambda+a_0
        </me>
        be the characteristic polynomial of the associated homogeneous ODE.
        The following table gives the standard trial function for <m>y_p(t)</m>.
        The constants named <m>A</m> or <m>A_j</m> are known from the given
        right-hand side; the constants named <m>B</m> or <m>B_j</m> are unknown
        and will be determined by substitution.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the table, the <q>root to check</q> is the complex number
        that represents the same exponential or oscillating shape as the
        forcing term.  If that number is a root of <m>P</m> with multiplicity
        <m>k</m>, multiply the natural trial function by <m>t^k</m>.  If it is not a
        root, use <m>k=0</m>, so no extra factor of <m>t</m> is added.
      </p>
      <tabular halign="left" top="medium" bottom="medium">
        <col right="medium"/>
        <col right="medium"/>
        <col/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Right-hand side <m>f(t)</m></cell>
          <cell>Root to check</cell>
          <cell>Trial function for <m>y_p(t)</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Polynomial <m>\displaystyle\sum_{j=0}^{m} A_j t^j</m></cell>
          <cell><m>0</m></cell>
          <cell><m>t^k\displaystyle\sum_{j=0}^{m} B_j t^j</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><m>Ae^{\alpha t}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\alpha</m></cell>
          <cell><m>B\,t^k e^{\alpha t}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><m>A_1\cos(\beta t)+A_2\sin(\beta t)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>i\beta</m> and <m>-i\beta</m></cell>
          <cell><m>t^k\!\left(B_1\cos(\beta t)+B_2\sin(\beta t)\right)</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell><m>e^{\alpha t}\!\left(A_1\cos(\beta t)+A_2\sin(\beta t)\right)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\alpha+i\beta</m> and <m>\alpha-i\beta</m></cell>
          <cell><m>t^k e^{\alpha t}\!\left(B_1\cos(\beta t)+B_2\sin(\beta t)\right)</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
      <p>
        For a sum of several forcing terms, choose one trial function for each
        term and add those trial functions together.  If the ODE has real coefficients,
        complex conjugate roots have the same multiplicity, so checking one
        member of a conjugate pair determines the same value of <m>k</m> for
        the other.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <remark xml:id="rem-guessing-method-scope">
    <title>Scope of the Method</title>
    <p>
      The method of undetermined coefficients works well when the forcing term
      <m>f(t)</m> is built from polynomials, exponentials, sines, cosines, or
      finite sums and products of these functions.  It is not a universal
      method for arbitrary right-hand sides such as <m>\ln t</m> or
      <m>\tan t</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      That scope restriction is useful to keep in mind: in this section we
      deliberately stay within the constant-coefficient setting where the
      forcing term suggests a manageable trial function.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <remark xml:id="rem-resonance-explanation">
    <title>Why the Resonance Factor <m>t^k</m>?</title>
    <p>
      The case <m>k \gt 0</m> is called <term>resonance</term>.  Algebraically,
      it means that the natural trial function for <m>y_p</m> overlaps with a solution
      of the associated homogeneous ODE.  If we try that overlapping trial function,
      the homogeneous left-hand side either becomes zero or loses exactly the
      shape we need, so it cannot produce the required forcing term.
      Multiplying by <m>t</m> moves the trial function to the next related function
      shape; if the same characteristic root occurs <m>k</m> times, we must
      move <m>k</m> steps and multiply by <m>t^k</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This phenomenon has a physical counterpart: when a driving force
      matches the natural frequency of an undamped oscillator the amplitude
      grows without bound (resonance).
    </p>
  </remark>

  <!-- ─── Worked example 1: polynomial RHS ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-inhomogeneous-polynomial-rhs">
    <title>Inhomogeneous ODE — Polynomial Right-Hand Side</title>
    <p>
      Find the general solution of
      <m>y''(t) - 2y'(t) + y(t) = t^2 + 4</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 1 — Homogeneous solution.</em>
      We found earlier (<xref ref="ex-homogeneous-double-real-root"/>)
      that the characteristic polynomial is
      <m>P(\lambda) = (\lambda - 1)^2</m>
      and the general homogeneous solution is
      <me>
        y_h(t) = C_1\, e^t + C_2\, t\, e^t.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 2 — Particular solution.</em>
      The right-hand side is a polynomial of degree 2.
      Check for resonance: <m>P(0) = (0-1)^2 = 1 \neq 0</m>, so
      <m>\lambda = 0</m> is not a root and <m>k = 0</m>.
      Trial function: <m>y_p(t) = B_0 + B_1 t + B_2 t^2</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Compute the derivatives and substitute into the left-hand side:
      <md>
        <mrow>y_p'(t) \amp= B_1 + 2B_2 t,</mrow>
        <mrow>y_p''(t) \amp= 2B_2.</mrow>
      </md>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          y_p'' - 2y_p' + y_p
          \amp= 2B_2 - 2(B_1 + 2B_2 t) + (B_0 + B_1 t + B_2 t^2)
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp= (B_0 - 2B_1 + 2B_2)
               + (-4B_2 + B_1)\, t
               + B_2\, t^2.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      Equating coefficients with <m>f(t) = t^2 + 4 = 4 + 0\cdot t + 1\cdot t^2</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>B_2 \amp= 1,</mrow>
        <mrow>-4B_2 + B_1 \amp= 0 \;\Rightarrow\; B_1 = 4,</mrow>
        <mrow>B_0 - 2B_1 + 2B_2 \amp= 4 \;\Rightarrow\; B_0 = 10.</mrow>
      </md>
      Thus <m>y_p(t) = 10 + 4t + t^2</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 3 — General solution.</em>
      <me>
        y(t) = C_1\, e^t + C_2\, t\, e^t + t^2 + 4t + 10,
        \qquad C_1, C_2 \in \R.
      </me>
    </p>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Worked example 2: resonance with exponential ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-inhomogeneous-exponential-resonance">
    <title>Inhomogeneous ODE — Resonance with Exponential Forcing</title>
    <p>
      Find the general solution of
      <m>y''(t) + 6y'(t) + 9y(t) = 3e^{-3t}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 1 — Homogeneous solution.</em>
      <m>P(\lambda) = \lambda^2 + 6\lambda + 9 = (\lambda + 3)^2</m>.
      Root <m>\lambda_0 = -3</m> has multiplicity 2, so
      <me>
        y_h(t) = C_1\, e^{-3t} + C_2\, t\, e^{-3t}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 2 — Particular solution.</em>
      The right-hand side is <m>f(t) = 3\, e^{-3t}</m>, so
      <m>\alpha = -3</m>.
      Since <m>\lambda = -3</m> is a root of <m>P</m> of multiplicity
      <m>k = 2</m>, we use the resonance trial function
      <m>y_p(t) = B\, t^2 e^{-3t}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Compute derivatives (using the product rule twice):
      <md>
        <mrow>y_p' \amp= B(2t - 3t^2) e^{-3t},</mrow>
        <mrow>y_p'' \amp= B(2 - 12t + 9t^2) e^{-3t}.</mrow>
      </md>
      Substituting into the ODE:
      <md>
        <mrow>
          y_p'' + 6y_p' + 9y_p
          \amp= B e^{-3t}\!\left[(2 - 12t + 9t^2)
               + 6(2t - 3t^2) + 9t^2\right]
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp= B e^{-3t}\!\left[2 + 0\cdot t + 0\cdot t^2\right]
            = 2B\, e^{-3t}.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      Setting <m>2B = 3</m> gives <m>B = \tfrac{3}{2}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 3 — General solution.</em>
      <me>
        y(t) = C_1\, e^{-3t} + C_2\, t\, e^{-3t}
               + \tfrac{3}{2}\, t^2 e^{-3t},
        \qquad C_1, C_2 \in \R.
      </me>
    </p>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Worked example 3: complex exponential forcing ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-inhomogeneous-complex-exponential-forcing">
    <title>Inhomogeneous ODE — Exponentially Modulated Oscillation</title>
    <p>
      Find the general solution of
      <m>y''(t) + 2y'(t) + 5y(t) = e^{-3t}\sin(2t)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 1 — Homogeneous solution.</em>
      <m>P(\lambda) = \lambda^2 + 2\lambda + 5</m>.
      Roots: <m>\lambda_{1,2} = -1 \pm 2i</m>.
      <me>
        y_h(t) = e^{-t}\!\left(C_1 \cos(2t) + C_2 \sin(2t)\right).
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 2 — Particular solution.</em>
      The right-hand side has <m>\alpha = -3</m>, <m>\beta = 2</m>.
      The complex frequency <m>\alpha + i\beta = -3 + 2i</m> is
      <em>not</em> a root of <m>P</m> (since the roots are <m>-1 \pm 2i</m>),
      so <m>k = 0</m> and the trial function is
      <me>
        y_p(t) = e^{-3t}\!\left(B_1 \cos(2t) + B_2 \sin(2t)\right).
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Let <m>u(t) = B_1\cos(2t) + B_2\sin(2t)</m>.  After computing
      <m>y_p''+ 2y_p' + 5y_p</m> via the product rule and comparing
      coefficients with <m>e^{-3t}\sin(2t)</m>, one obtains the system
      <md>
        <mrow>-3 B_1 + 2 B_2 \amp= 0,</mrow>
        <mrow>-2 B_1 - 3 B_2 \amp= 1.</mrow>
      </md>
      Solving: <m>B_1 = -\tfrac{2}{13}</m>, <m>B_2 = -\tfrac{3}{13}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 3 — General solution.</em>
      <me>
        y(t) = e^{-t}\!\left(C_1\cos 2t + C_2\sin 2t\right)
               - \tfrac{e^{-3t}}{13}\!\left(2\cos 2t + 3\sin 2t\right),
        \quad C_1, C_2 \in \R.
      </me>
    </p>
  </example>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-inhomogeneous-higher-order-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-inhomogeneous-guess-polynomial">
      <title>Choose a Trial Function</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For the ODE
          <me>
            y''-y=\sin t,
          </me>
          which trial function is appropriate for a particular solution?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>y_p(t)=A\cos t+B\sin t</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Correct. A sine forcing generally requires both sine and cosine terms, because derivatives of sine and cosine produce each other.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p(t)=A\sin t</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. This is the common incomplete trial function: after differentiating, cosine terms can appear, so both sine and cosine should be included.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p(t)=At+B</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. Polynomial trial functions are used for polynomial forcing terms, not sinusoidal forcing.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p(t)=Ae^t</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. Exponential trial functions are used for exponential forcing terms.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-inhomogeneous-resonance-exp">
      <title>Find a Particular Solution in a Resonance Case</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find one particular solution of
          <me>
            y''-2y'+y=e^t.
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The homogeneous characteristic polynomial is
          <me>
            (\lambda-1)^2,
          </me>
          so <m>e^t</m> is already a homogeneous solution. We therefore use the resonance trial function
          <me>
            y_p(t)=At^2e^t.
          </me>
          Substituting gives
          <me>
            y_p''-2y_p'+y_p=2Ae^t.
          </me>
          To match the right-hand side <m>e^t</m>, we choose <m>2A=1</m>, hence <m>A=\tfrac12</m>.
          Thus one particular solution is
          <me>
            y_p(t)=\tfrac12 t^2 e^t.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</subsection>
